# Put Jenkins behind HTTPS with Caddy — the durable fix for the browser's
# "insecure download blocked" warning (Chrome/Brave refuse .zip/.xlsx downloads
# served over plain HTTP). Once Jenkins is a secure (https) origin, EVERY
# download works, including the htmlpublisher "Zip" button.
#
# Caddy fetches + renews TLS certificates automatically. Pick ONE site block
# below, edit the address, then:
#     caddy run --config ci/jenkins/https/Caddyfile
# (or `caddy start` to run detached; `caddy reload` after edits).
#
# After it's up, set Jenkins' root URL to the https address:
#   Manage Jenkins → System → Jenkins URL → https://<your-address>/

# ── Option A: LAN / homelab, no public DNS (self-signed, internal CA) ─────────
# Serve on the machine's IP or a .local/.lan name with a locally-trusted cert.
# Browsers will prompt to trust Caddy's local CA the first time (accept once).
https://10.0.0.8 {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8080
	tls internal
	encode gzip zstd
}

# ── Option B: real domain with automatic public certificates ──────────────────
# Uncomment and set your domain + email; Caddy provisions a Let's Encrypt cert.
# ci.example.com {
# 	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8080
# 	encode gzip zstd
# 	tls you@example.com
# }
